---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/url';
import { SETUP_BUDGETS, setupTotal, moneyRange } from '../../data/setup-budgets';

const cost = SETUP_BUDGETS[40];

const headline = "40 Gallon Breeder Reef Tank: Setup, Gear List & Cost";
---
<Base
  headline={headline}
  title="40 Gallon Breeder Reef Tank Setup: Gear & Cost"
  description="Build a 40 gallon breeder reef tank with a sized gear list and cost estimates. Compare pumps, skimmers, lights, flow, and beginner stocking choices."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { name: '40 Gallon Breeder Setup', href: '/guides/40-gallon-breeder-reef-tank-setup' }]}
>
  <section class="container prose" style="padding-top:40px;">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--brand-2)">Setup guide</p>
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="lede-dark">The 40-gallon breeder ("40B") is the most-recommended first reef for a reason: a wide 18-inch footprint to aquascape, low height for easy lighting, and a size that's forgiving without being expensive. Here's exactly what goes into one — and how to size each piece for <em>your</em> build, not a generic list.</p>

    <Disclosure />

    <h2>Why the 40 breeder?</h2>
    <p>At 36 × 18 × 16 inches, the 40B gives you the front-to-back depth that makes aquascaping and coral placement actually work — something taller "40-tall" or narrow tanks can't. The low height means even mid-range lights punch to the sand bed, and the ~40-gallon volume is stable enough to forgive beginner mistakes while staying cheap to stock and run. Allow for temperature and salinity checks even on a larger tank.</p>

    <h2>The core gear list</h2>
    <p>A reef tank is a system, and each part has to match the others. Here's the short version, with the two pieces people most often get wrong linked to our calculators.</p>

    <h3>Sump + return pump</h3>
    <p>Run a sump — it hides equipment, adds water volume, and holds your skimmer. A 20-gallon long fits most 40B stands. For the return pump, aim for roughly <strong>7× your display volume per hour at the tank</strong> (~280 GPH for a 40B) — but the pump's box rating is measured at zero head, and your real plumbing eats a big chunk of that. Don't guess:</p>
    <p><a href={url('/tools/return-pump-calculator')} class="inlinecta">→ Size your return pump with the calculator</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:-6px;"><a href={url('/reviews/best-return-pumps')}>See our full return pump rankings →</a></p>

    <h3>Protein skimmer</h3>
    <p>The skimmer is your main filtration. Manufacturer "rated for X gallons" numbers are optimistic — they assume light stocking. For a typical mixed-reef 40B you want a skimmer that's honestly rated at your volume once derated for bioload:</p>
    <p><a href={url('/tools/protein-skimmer-calculator')} class="inlinecta">→ Find the right skimmer size for your bioload</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:-6px;"><a href={url('/reviews/best-protein-skimmers')}>See our full protein skimmer rankings →</a></p>

    <h3>Lighting</h3>
    <p>Light depends on what you'll keep. Softies and LPS are happy under a single quality reef LED; a mixed-to-SPS 40B usually runs two fixtures for even coverage across the 36-inch length. Buy for the corals you want in a year, not just today. See <a href={url('/reviews/best-reef-led-lighting')}>our reef LED lighting rankings</a> — check the source conditions before comparing any recorded PAR numbers.</p>

    <h3>Flow, heat, rock</h3>
    <p>Add two small powerheads for turbulent, random flow (aim for 20–40× total turnover including the return — <a href={url('/tools/flow-calculator')}>the flow calculator</a> does the math and names the pumps). A 100–150W heater on an external controller keeps things stable (<a href={url('/tools/heater-calculator')}>size it for your room</a>). Around 35 lb of dry or live rock builds your aquascape and biological base.</p>

    <h2>What a 40B build actually costs</h2>
    <p>Two honest columns — a value build and a mid-range build. These are editorial US cost estimates, not refreshed retailer quotes, not exact prices; use the calculators above to pin down the pump and skimmer for your setup, and <a href={url('/tools/reef-build-cost-calculator')}>the full build-cost calculator</a> to itemize your own tier and coral type. Salt is the other recurring line — see <a href={url('/reviews/best-reef-salt-mix')}>our salt cost-per-gallon rankings</a> to pick one.</p>

    <table class="costtable">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Value build</th><th>Mid-range</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {cost.map((c) => (<tr><td>{c.item}</td><td>{moneyRange(c.budget)}</td><td>{moneyRange(c.mid)}</td></tr>))}
      </tbody>
      <tfoot><tr><td><strong>Listed gear total</strong></td><td><strong>{moneyRange(setupTotal(40, 'budget'))}</strong></td><td><strong>{moneyRange(setupTotal(40, 'mid'))}</strong></td></tr></tfoot>
    </table>
    <p class="rnote">Spread it out: you don't buy everything at once. Tank, sump, RODI, and rock come first; livestock and corals come months later, after the tank cycles and stabilizes.</p>

    <h2>The order of operations</h2>
    <p>Set the tank and plumb the sump → run RODI and mix saltwater → aquascape the rock → start the pump, skimmer, and heater → cycle the tank (2–6 weeks) → add a cleanup crew → then fish, then corals, slowly. Patience is the cheapest equipment upgrade in the hobby.</p>

    <h2>Other sizes</h2>
    <p>Tighter space or budget? <a href={url('/guides/30-gallon-reef-tank-setup')}>The 30</a> is the smallest size we'd call forgiving. Same idea, more room: <a href={url('/guides/75-gallon-reef-tank-setup')}>the 75</a> keeps the 18-inch depth across four feet — and if a cheap 55 is tempting you, <a href={url('/guides/55-gallon-reef-tank-setup')}>read this first</a>. Going big: <a href={url('/guides/120-gallon-reef-tank-setup')}>the 120</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <EmailCapture />
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede-dark { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .inlinecta { display:inline-block; font-family:var(--font-display); font-weight:700; color:var(--brand-2); }
  .costtable { width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:18px 0; font-size:.95rem; }
  .costtable th, .costtable td { text-align:left; padding:10px 12px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); }
  .costtable thead th { font-family:var(--font-display); color:var(--ink); border-bottom:2px solid var(--line); }
  .costtable tfoot td { border-top:2px solid var(--line); border-bottom:none; }
  .costtable td:nth-child(2), .costtable td:nth-child(3), .costtable th:nth-child(2), .costtable th:nth-child(3) { white-space:nowrap; color:var(--muted); }
</style>
